before I introduce mrs. Clinton I'd like  to begin by introducing congressman  Bernard Sanders of Vermont congressman  Sanders and I would like to introduce a  graduate of Yale who is a very good  friend  it was a very good friend of Dartmouth  and that's our congressman  representative dick sweat thank you  thank you very much don't take too much  time away from the first lady I want to  be very brief I do realize that Yale was  nice enough to let Dartmouth win this  year so but I want to say just just a  couple of things number one I want to  thank Dartmouth and especially president  Friedman for making this visit of the  first lady's so productive and so warm  and so welcoming and it's really a  tribute to the student population as  well as to the faculty and the  administration that you could set such a  wonderful place as the northern New  Hampshire town of hanover to welcome  someone who has done such tremendous  work in health care reform as the first  lady I have had a great deal of  experience with Yale it goes back a long  time but I've also had some experience  with the administration and you know i  might say with my tongue firmly planted  in my cheek that i have sometimes been a  thorn in the side of the administration  I want the first lady to understand I  love and respect everything that the  president and she has done they have  encouraged me to views open and very  honest debate and there's nothing better  for removing thorns and sides than a  little health care reform that's why I'm  announcing today that I'm going to be  the hundred and first co-sponsor of the  president's health care reform package  it's an honor to do so I'm very pleased  and proud to be associated with such  credible and important work that the  administration is carrying forward and  I'm very grateful for the opportunity to  be here with the first lady today to  listen to her tell you me and the rest  of the world why this is so important to  this country lastly for the press I will  be out on the balcony at Alumni Hall  right after this we can talk about my  decision and my plans at that time thank  you very much  in February 1992 mrs. Clinton spoke at  Dartmouth and into hindman forum in the  Rockefeller Center and announced on that  day that if governor clinton was elected  president she would return to speak at  dartmouth she is today keeping that  promise we are delighted to welcome mrs.  Clinton for a second time to Dartmouth  and we hope that you'll be here many  many more times besides Hillary Rodham  Clinton  thank you thank you all thank you so  much this is such a treat I had looked  forward to coming back to Dartmouth and  it gives me such a real thrill to be  here with you although I have to correct  president Friedman for the second time  today earlier earlier he introduced me  and said I had been at Dartmouth back in  February of 92 for my second visit and  that was my first visit this is my  second visit and I told the crowd then  that that doesn't count all the visits I  used to make to Dartmouth when I was at  Wellesley back in the back in the far  distant past when  in fact I remember I remember my very  first visit to Dartmouth I came up for  some kind of meeting that Dartmouth was  holding and I also came for winter  carnival but that was later this was for  the meeting and I came to the meeting  and the meeting was being run and we  were all being organized by Bobby Rice  who is still organizing America one of  your most distinguished alums and the  Secretary of Labor and I will never  forget we had this incredible discussion  and that was before there any women at  Dartmouth at least in the college and so  I must say that I had never had a better  time those of us who came to this  meeting that Bobby Rush put on because  there were about ten of us and about 400  of you also it was great but it is it's  a real treat to be back and especially  for this occasion because what we are  trying to do is to make good on what we  said during the campaign when so many of  you here at the college worked for my  husband's candidacy both in the primary  and then in the general election because  you shared with us what we saw as a real  historic opportunity a chance to begin  to deal with a lot of the problems in  our country a chance to have a new  vision of what it would mean to be a  strong productive America in the 21st  century how we could ensure that this  country which has given us all so much  would continue to provide the  opportunity that you deserve to have and  in the past year we have worked very  hard to build on that vision and we're  very proud of the record of legislative  accomplishment and the changes that have  already begun I don't think there is any  program that is dearer to the  president's heart and in which he feels  more promise than the national service  bill which was passed and signed into  law which will begin next year providing  opportunities for young people to do  national service and for thousands to  begin to finance their way through  college because they're willing to do  service to America that's what a new  America vision means but part of what  still lies before us  is to tackle a problem that is really  one that has developed over decades but  which threatens to undermine one of the  strongest parts of our national heritage  there isn't any doubt that America has  the finest medical system in the world  if you can afford to use it if you can  get there if you can take advantage of  what it has to offer there's also no  doubt that for those who are well  insured there is a certain sense of  being taken care of but increasingly  even for those of us who are insured  there is no security every month 2.25  million Americans lose their health  insurance and usually they lose it  because they change jobs or they're laid  off or the business they were working  for no longer will provide health care  but sometimes it's because all of a  sudden they can't be insured because  they've got an illness or a child has a  problem that nobody wants to take care  of and so even for those of us who are  insured there isn't any real health  security and what the president is  absolutely committed to doing is  providing health care reform that first  and foremost gives every one of you the  absolute confidence that no matter who  you work for no matter where you live no  matter whether you have a pre-existing  condition which is insurance language  for having been sick and maybe still  having something you have to take care  of like diabetes that you will always  always be able to afford to have your  health care needs met I'm very grateful  for the leadership that many people are  giving to this great reform effort and  I'm grateful that congressman Sanders  would join us today from Vermont and I  am particularly pleased the congressman  sweat would announce his endorsement of  the health care reform the health  security act today here at his home  state but you know there has been  efforts in the past to reform our health  care system  this is not a problem that is new back  in the days of your grandparents and  great-grandparents Franklin Roosevelt  thought that he would do social security  which would lift millions and millions  of Americans and their older years out  of abject poverty and then he would do  health security but he couldn't get it  done and then President Truman came  along and in 1945 he said we have to  make sure that every person in America  has health insurance no matter what  their race or their residents or their  economic statuses but he tried in 1945  he tried in 1947 and he couldn't get it  done because there were so many  interests that said why do we need to do  that we don't need to worry about that  now and then in the 1960s President  Johnson was able to get through both  Medicare and Medicaid to programs that  have made a huge difference in the lives  of millions of Americans many of your  grandparents my parents were on Medicare  which enabled them to deal and my  father's last days with very severe  hospitalization and medical costs and  knowing that they wouldn't have to sell  their house they wouldn't go bankrupt  they'd have to pay something but it  wasn't going to destroy what they had  saved all those years and now we know  that the changes that were made then  made this a more humane and better  society for the very poor and for the  elderly President Nixon tried to get  health care reform through in the early  1970s and again it didn't go anywhere  now is the time yes there will be great  discussion in our country for the next  month's and I'm thrilled to see some of  these signs where clearly people are and  the campuses around America becoming  involved and interested in what health  care security will mean to them I  remember very well when I was in college  I didn't think about health insurance it  wasn't something that was on the top of  my mind I didn't realize that you know I  might need it someday and it wasn't  really until I was married and probably  not really until I was apparent that I  began to understand what it would mean  to  if I didn't have health insurance and  what it means to millions of Americans  every day just want to tell you just a  very few little stories that may reflect  something of your own experience about  why this is important for everybody  because remember what we are trying to  do is not just to take care of those  people who get up every day and go to  work I bet some of your families fall  into this category but they don't make  quite enough money to be able to afford  insurance and their employers don't help  them they are uninsured yes we're going  to make sure every American is insured  but the only way we can do that is by  ensuring that every American always has  health care benefits I don't want in the  next year's to go around our country and  to talk to the people I've talked with  with the stories they have to tell me i  sat in the living room of a small  farmhouse in Iowa with four farm  families who opened their books to show  me what it costs them to try to keep  health insurance and try to make a  living on the farm there was one family  because the father had had an accident  on the farm and one of their children  had diabetes they were paying nine  thousand dollars a year for health  insurance that was as much as they made  from their farm on some years and I've  been in New Orleans talking to people  who've worked all their lives for the  same company but never could afford  insurance I've had a woman look at me  and say I've worked here 15 years and  I've always tried to take care of myself  and every year I go and get a physical  and I pay for it out of my own pocket  because I want to stay healthy but last  year she said the doctor found a lump in  my breast and sent me to the surgeon the  surgeon said you know if you had  insurance we would biopsy it but since  you don't why don't we just watch it and  I thought to myself what if that had  been me or my mother or my friend and  I've talked to small business people who  can't get insurance in today's market  without paying an exorbitant amount of  money because we discriminate against  small business people and I've looked at  a mother and a father who had left their  jobs where they had insurance because  they wanted to start a small business  but  they couldn't afford insurance and the  mother said she had told her two sons  don't play sports this year boys because  if he get hurt we can't afford it and  I've talked to another small business  person who ensures his family and his  four employees and their family and then  all of a sudden one of his longtime  employees had a child with Down syndrome  like the young man whose hand I shook as  I was coming through the corridor  they're working here making his  contribution to Dartmouth but this man  when he was faced with the insurance  company that said you know we're gonna  have to raise your rates really high  because you've got this employee with a  Down Syndrome child and my friend said  well what can I do about that and the  agent said way you can fire him that  shouldn't be a choice anybody has to  make those are the stories that are made  every day in America that move us to say  we can do better than this we know this  is the smartest richest strongest best  nation in the world in solving problems  and it's about time we put our minds to  work and solve this one and once we  solve it we will have provided health  security for every American no matter  who they are where they live who they  work for or whatever happens to them but  we would have done something else we  would have made a statement about what  our values are as a people we would have  said that you know it's time we got back  to taking care of each other in this  country it's time we viewed each other  as members of a great extended community  and where some of us maybe can help out  a little bit to make sure people get  taken care of we want to do that because  there but for the grace of God go you or  I so this is a debate about health care  and i hope you will read this book which  is going to be in the library will be in  the bookstore and i'll give this to  president friedman to have available but  more than that i hope you will think  about what we are trying to achieve in  our country and what opportunities there  are for your generation to be engaged in  making sure we are always always on the  cutting edge of change and promise and  possibility  that America lives up to its promise to  you the way it did for me and my husband  thank you all very much  mrs. Clinton on behalf of all of  Dartmouth students I'd like to present  you with this gift  thank you  well thank you president Friedman and I  want to give you these copies and I want  to assure you that we cannot get this  done without the big green behind us so  you all got to be there

